CHECK:UNNECESSARY_PARENTHESES: Unnecessary parentheses around 'wdd->ops->set_timeout == NULL'
#127: FILE: drivers/watchdog/core/watchdog_dev.c:227:
+		if ((wdd->ops->set_timeout == NULL) ||
+		    !(wdd->info->options & WDIOF_SETTIMEOUT))

Parentheses are not required in the following cases:

  1. Function pointer uses::

      (foo->bar)();

    could be::

      foo->bar();

  2. Comparisons in if::

      if ((foo->bar) && (foo->baz))
      if ((foo == bar))

    could be::

      if (foo->bar && foo->baz)
      if (foo == bar)

  3. addressof/dereference single Lvalues::

      &(foo->bar)
      *(foo->bar)

    could be::

      &foo->bar
      *foo->bar

CHECK:COMPARISON_TO_NULL: Comparison to NULL could be written "!wdd->ops->set_timeout"
#127: FILE: drivers/watchdog/core/watchdog_dev.c:227:
+		if ((wdd->ops->set_timeout == NULL) ||

Comparisons to NULL in the form (foo == NULL) or (foo != NULL)
are better written as (!foo) and (foo).

WARNING:BLOCK_COMMENT_STYLE: Block comments use a trailing */ on a separate line
#138: FILE: drivers/watchdog/core/watchdog_dev.c:238:
+		 * possible that it takes the new timeout) */

The comment style is incorrect.  The preferred style for multi-
line comments is::

  /*
  * This is the preferred style
  * for multi line comments.
  */

The networking comment style is a bit different, with the first line
not empty like the former::

  /* This is the preferred comment style
  * for files in net/ and drivers/net/
  */

See: https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/process/coding-style.html#commenting

WARNING:FUNCTION_ARGUMENTS: function definition argument 'struct watchdog_device *' should also have an identifier name
#156: FILE: include/linux/watchdog.h:74:
+	int (*set_timeout)(struct watchdog_device *, int);

This warning is emitted due to any of the following reasons:

  1. Arguments for the function declaration do not follow
     the identifier name.  Example::

       void foo
       (int bar, int baz)

     This should be corrected to::

       void foo(int bar, int baz)

  2. Some arguments for the function definition do not
     have an identifier name.  Example::

       void foo(int)

     All arguments should have identifier names.

